  "honest_take": "The upgrades over the Pro are real and address the Pro's actual weak points rather than being cosmetic: the Pro has no SD card and about a third of the Ultra's claimed battery life, so if you found the Pro's runtime or lack of offline logging frustrating, the Ultra specifically fixes that. But it inherits every one of the Pro's overclaims: 'dual-band scanning 2.4 and 5GHz simultaneously' is not possible with the C5's single radio (it hops channels, per the vendor's own wiki), the WROOM-32E's Bluetooth is 4.2 per Espressif's datasheet despite marketing claiming 'BLE 5.0 + Classic', and there is still no screen, no GPS, no sub-GHz, no NFC and no IR. Same closed firmware, same single-maintainer risk, and a 'do not buy if you have a Nothing 4a pro' compatibility warning on the vendor's own page that should give any buyer pause about how broadly this has been tested against real phones.",
  "best_for": [
   "5GHz Wi-Fi recon with meaningfully longer runtime and offline SD logging than the Pro",
   "Multi-node distributed sensor rigs, using the Ultra as a coordinator for external C5 nodes",
   "Long unattended wardriving sessions where the Pro's battery and lack of SD card were limiting"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Anyone who wants a self-contained device - there is still no screen; it does nothing without the phone app",
   "Sub-GHz, NFC, RFID or infrared - none of that exists here either",
   "Buying today - sold out with an unclear restock date",
   "Nothing 4a Pro owners - the vendor itself warns of unstable BLE with that specific phone"
  ],
