  "category": "network-implant",
  "subcategory": "autonomous-lan-attacker",
  "status": "diy-only",
  "released": "2024",
  "price_usd": 70.0,
  "price_note": "ESTIMATE, parts only. Raspberry Pi Zero W or Zero 2 W ~$15-25, Waveshare 2.13in e-Paper HAT ~$25-30 (v2 and v4 are the tested revisions - buy the right one, the panel revisions are not interchangeable), microSD 16GB+ ~$8, USB power bank or LiPo HAT ~$12-20, case ~$10. Realistic all-in $70-90. It needs an Ethernet or Wi-Fi route onto the target LAN, so on a Pi Zero W add a USB Ethernet adapter ~$8 if you want to plug it in.",
  "price_is_estimate": true,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "The install script does most of the work, but it insists on a specific OS image, a username and hostname of 'bjorn', and a matching e-Paper panel revision - get any of those wrong and you get a blank screen with no useful error.",
  "build_effort": "assembly",
  "build_note": "Stack the e-Paper HAT on the Pi, flash Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm (32-bit for Zero W, 64-bit for Zero 2 W), set the username and hostname to 'bjorn', run the installer. No soldering with a pre-headered Pi.",
  "stealth": "discreet",
  "form_factor": "pocket",
  "summary": "A Raspberry Pi with a 2.13in e-ink screen that autonomously works a network: it discovers live hosts, scans ports, fingerprints services, runs Nmap vulnerability scripts, then brute-forces FTP, SSH, SMB, RDP, Telnet and SQL logins and pulls files off anything it gets into. A Viking character on the display gains 'levels' as it succeeds, which is the hook that made it popular.",
  "honest_take": "Be very clear what this is: it is not a passive collector like a Pwnagotchi, it is an autonomous credential brute-forcer that steals files, and running it on a network you do not own is straightforwardly a crime in most countries - the cute Viking does not change that. Technically there is nothing here you could not do with a shell script wrapping nmap and hydra; what Bjorn adds is the orchestration, the e-ink UI and the game loop. It also brute-forces loudly, so any network with account lockout, fail2ban or basic monitoring will catch it almost immediately. The 6,000+ stars measure how good the idea looks in a demo video, not how useful it is on a real engagement.",
  "best_for": [
   "A home-lab exercise in what an unattended attacker does to a flat network with default credentials",
   "Learning how host discovery, service fingerprinting and credential attacks chain together",
   "Demonstrating to non-technical people why default passwords matter"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Any network you do not own or have written authorisation to test",
   "Quiet or evasive testing - it is extremely noisy",
   "Wi-Fi attacks; it works on the network it is already attached to",
   "Anything requiring modern exploitation rather than guessed credentials"
  ],
