  "honest_take": "Where it still fits is documentation, not capability. If you are following an older write-up or a training course that says 'connect your Bus Pirate and type m for mode', it means this one, and the command syntax carried over only partly to the Bus Pirate 5. It is also cheap enough to be disposable. Everything else argues for the newer board: 3.3 V logic with no 1.8 V support at all is a real problem on modern targets, it is much slower, and the firmware has not meaningfully moved in years. Buy it as a second cheap unit or to follow old tutorials; do not buy it as your only tool.",
