  "summary": "A DIY, from-scratch CMSIS-DAP debug probe built around the CH552, an 8-bit 8051-core microcontroller from WCH (Nanjing Qinheng) that costs under $1 in single quantities. It implements SWD and JTAG plus a virtual COM port, making it one of the cheapest possible ways to get a working ARM Cortex debug probe if you're willing to solder and compile it yourself.",
  "honest_take": "The headline appeal is the sub-$1 chip, but the CH552's 8051 core is doing everything - CMSIS-DAP framing, SWD/JTAG bit-banging and the USB stack - on an architecture with none of the bit-banging throughput of an ARM Cortex-M0+ (like the RP2040 in a Mini DAPLink) or the LPC11U35 in an IBDAP. Expect it to be the slowest probe in this comparison, not the cheapest-per-performance one; nobody publishes a benchmark number for it, which is itself telling. It also has no I2C, SPI flash-dumping or glitching capability - it is SWD/JTAG plus UART, nothing more. It is a genuinely good learning project - real open firmware you compile yourself, on real Cortex-M silicon - but not a build you'd choose over a $4.80 pre-assembled Mini DAPLink if raw debugging speed or your own time matters.",
