  "summary": "Cypher NFC is an open-source, single-maintainer Arduino project that turns an ESP32-C3 + PN532 + SSD1306 OLED + SD card into a pocket NFC workstation: it reads and dumps MIFARE Classic/Ultralight/NTAG cards, runs an onboard dictionary attack against MIFARE Classic keys, clones Gen1a magic cards, and can emulate a Type 4 NDEF tag from a saved dump. A companion sketch ports the same logic to the M5Stack Cardputer ADV and a separate 'Cypherbox Mini' build.",
  "honest_take": "This is a hobbyist Arduino sketch wired to off-the-shelf modules (PN532, SSD1306, generic SD reader) you can buy on Amazon for well under $25 total - there's no finished product, case, or kit sold; you solder and wire it yourself. Functionally it's a slower, more limited clone of what a Flipper Zero or Proxmark3 already does for MIFARE Classic: the PN532 chip itself can't emulate a MIFARE Classic target (only NTAG/Type4 NDEF), and there's no MIFARE DESFire, ISO15693, or EMV support at all. It's maintained by one person (48 stars, 10 forks, 5 open issues), and the built-in Wi-Fi control mode runs an unauthenticated AP at 192.168.4.1 - don't leave it powered on an open network mid-attack.",
