  "price_note": "ESTIMATE. Digispark ATtiny85 clones run about $1.50-3 each on AliExpress, usually cheaper in 5- or 10-packs; CH552G and CH552T boards run about $2-5. This is the reference point people mean by 'the $5 alternative to a Rubber Ducky' - roughly a fortieth of a Hak5 USB Rubber Ducky. You get a bare board with a USB-A tongue and nothing else: no case, no storage, no wireless, no support.",
  "price_is_estimate": true,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "Cheap to buy, fiddly to use. On Digispark you fight the micronucleus bootloader, Windows driver installation via Zadig, and a plug-in timing window; on CH552 the flashing is easier but the toolchain is less familiar. Neither is a plug-and-play experience.",
  "build_effort": "flash-only",
  "build_note": "No soldering - buy the board, install the toolchain, compile a sketch with your payload, flash. The Digispark's micronucleus flow requires you to unplug the board, start the upload and then plug it in within a few seconds.",
  "stealth": "discreet",
  "form_factor": "usb-stick",
  "summary": "The budget end of keystroke injection: two families of very cheap microcontroller boards with a USB-A plug on the PCB, flashed with a sketch that makes them enumerate as a keyboard and type a payload. The Digispark uses an ATtiny85 with software USB; CH552 boards use a WCH 8051 microcontroller with a real hardware USB peripheral. Together they are the standard answer to 'do I really need a $80 Rubber Ducky'.",
  "honest_take": "For most people, no, you do not need the expensive one - but know what you are giving up. There is no storage on either, so the payload is compiled into the firmware and changing it means reflashing, and there is no exfiltration path at all. And prefer CH552 over Digispark: the ATtiny85 bit-bangs USB in software (V-USB, low-speed only), which is why Digisparks are notorious for failing to enumerate on USB 3.x ports and hubs, for needing driver wrangling on Windows, and for the five-second bootloader delay before your payload runs. The CH552 has a hardware full-speed USB peripheral and roughly twice the flash for about the same money. Also note the ecosystem here is quiet: the big Digispark script collections were last touched in 2022-2023. Finally, if a seller markets one of these as a 'hacking device' at $25, you are paying $22 for a sticker.",
  "best_for": [
   "Learning keystroke injection for the price of a coffee",
   "Disposable payload delivery where losing the device does not matter",
   "Buying ten and giving them out at a workshop"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Exfiltrating files - there is no storage on either family",
   "Changing payloads in the field",
   "Digispark specifically on USB 3.x-only machines, where enumeration is unreliable",
   "Anything needing wireless triggering"
  ],
  "hardware": {
   "mcu_primary": "ATtiny85 (Digispark) or CH552G / CH552T (WCH)",
   "mcu_primary_note": "ATtiny85: 8KB flash of which roughly 6KB is usable once the micronucleus bootloader is installed, 512B RAM, and NO hardware USB - USB is bit-banged in software via V-USB at low speed only. CH552: WCH's enhanced 8051 core, 16KB program flash, with a real hardware full-speed USB 2.0 device peripheral. That difference in USB implementation is the single most important fact about these two families.",
   "mcu_secondary": null,
   "radios": [],
   "display": {
    "type": "None",
    "size_in": null,
    "resolution": null,
    "color": false,
    "touch": false
   },
   "battery_mah": null,
   "battery_note": "None - USB powered.",
   "charging": "n/a",
   "pmic": null,
   "storage": "None on either family. The payload lives in program flash and is replaced by reflashing.",
   "connectivity": [
    "USB (HID)"
   ],
   "gpio": "A few broken-out pins on both board families, usable for an LED or a trigger switch",
   "sensors": [
    "none"
   ],
   "dimensions_mm": null,
   "weight_g": null,
   "case": "None - bare PCB with an exposed USB-A tongue",
   "notable_physical": [
    "USB-A contacts etched directly into the PCB edge, which is thinner than a real plug and fits loosely in some ports",
    "Digispark boards carry an onboard LED often used as a payload progress indicator"
   ]
  },
  "capabilities": {
   "usb_hid_inject": {
    "v": "yes",
    "note": "Both families enumerate as a USB HID keyboard. On CH552 this uses the hardware USB peripheral; on Digispark it is software-timed V-USB, which is where the reliability problems come from."
   },
   "usb_payload_lang": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Not native DuckyScript - you compile an Arduino sketch. Converters (Duckyspark, rubberDigi and similar) translate DuckyScript into a sketch, and community repositories carry hundreds of ready-made scripts. Keyboard-layout handling is manual."
   },
   "usb_mass_storage": {
    "v": "no",
    "note": "Notable absence and the main reason to spend more elsewhere: neither chip has the flash or the peripheral for it."
   },
   "usb_exfil": {
    "v": "no",
    "note": "No storage and no network. Anything stolen has to leave over the host's own connection, arranged entirely by the typed payload."
   },
   "hid_remote_trigger": {
    "v": "no",
    "note": "No radio of any kind. It fires on insertion, after the bootloader delay."
   },
   "scriptable": {
    "v": "partial",
    "note": "Only in the sense that you edit and recompile. There is no on-device scripting, no editor, and no way to change behaviour without a host and a toolchain."
   }
  },
  "legal": {
   "import_restricted": [],
   "risk_level": "medium",
   "notes": "These are ordinary hobbyist development boards, sold openly and legal to own everywhere. What makes them a weapon is the payload you compile in. Injecting keystrokes into a computer you do not own or lack authorisation to test is unauthorised access under the CFAA in the US, the Computer Misuse Act in the UK and equivalents elsewhere - the low price does not lower the stakes. Check your local law; this is not legal advice."
  },
