  "skill_note": "JST-connector wiring harness and the combined Cinder Ferret V2 board reduce this from a loose-module soldering project to mostly plugging harnesses together, per the included PDF build guide.",
  "build_effort": "assembly",
  "build_note": "8-pin and 12-pin JST wiring harness connects the display, Cinder Ferret V2 (CC1101+NRF24 combined) board, and GPS module into a 3D-printed case with an embedded NTAG216 tag. Includes boost and 3.3V buck converters and M3x16 hardware.",
  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "handheld",
  "summary": "The 3.5-inch DIY HaleHound kit: a QDtech E32R35T exposed-SPI CYD touchscreen, a 3D-printed case with an embedded NTAG216 passive NFC tag, a combined CC1101+NRF24 'Cinder Ferret V2' companion board, an ATGM336H GPS with ceramic antenna, and a JST wiring harness - running the same HaleHound firmware as the rest of the line, without a battery or the PN532 NFC reader.",
  "honest_take": "The embedded NTAG216 in the case is a passive tag, not a reader - without the PN532 this kit no longer includes, the firmware itself cannot read or write any NFC/RFID card; the NTAG216 is presumably there for something like tap-to-identify the unit, not for adding NFC capability to the toolset. That is a meaningful, easy-to-miss downgrade from the Complete Unit above and from the base bare-CYD HaleHound build documented elsewhere in this database, both of which do include a PN532. What you are paying $149 for over that bare-board approach (documented there at roughly $45-65 all-in for the same sub-GHz/2.4GHz/Wi-Fi/BLE feature set) is the combined Cinder Ferret V2 board and pre-made harness replacing loose-module soldering, plus a case and a bundled GPS - genuinely less DIY risk, at roughly 2-3x the parts cost. The firmware itself carries every caveat documented on the base entry: closed source, one maintainer, one flasher domain, 300-439MHz sub-GHz ceiling.",
  "best_for": [
   "Buyers who want the HaleHound Wi-Fi/BLE/sub-GHz/2.4GHz/GPS feature set without hand-soldering loose modules or a PN532",
   "A cheaper 3.5in alternative to the $235 hand-assembled Complete Unit, if you're willing to build it yourself"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Any NFC/RFID capability - the PN532 is no longer included and the embedded NTAG216 is a passive tag, not a reader",
   "868/915MHz sub-GHz targets",
   "Anyone who wants a battery in the box - none included"
  ],
