  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "usb-stick",
  "summary": "A dual-band, Wi-Fi 6-capable USB adapter built on MediaTek's MT7921AU chipset, sold by Hacker Warehouse as an unbranded/generic listing (no manufacturer named). Physically it's a small USB dongle body with two detachable external omni-directional antennas, using the same mt76/mt7921u Linux driver family as the internal uConsole card (device 2 in this batch). The listing claims monitor mode and packet injection support across 802.11 a/g/n/ac.",
  "honest_take": "This is a generic, likely white-label board - Hacker Warehouse doesn't name a manufacturer, and the listing's own disclaimer that you might receive a device that looks different from the picture is a real red flag for a security tool where you want to know exactly what silicon you're plugging in. Chipset-wise it's the same MT7921-series family as the uConsole card above, sharing the same mt7921u driver and the same open reliability problems: morrownr/USB-WiFi issue #387 documents frame injection failing or falling back to 1/6Mbps on this exact chipset, and openwrt/mt76 issue #839 documents active monitor mode (needed for PMKID capture) being broken on mt7921u. Whether 'MT7921AU' here is the identical die to the 'MT7921AUN' in device 2, or a genuinely distinct SKU, could not be confirmed from public sources - even the morrownr/USB-WiFi maintainers have an open, unresolved GitHub thread about it. Of the three Wi-Fi adapters in this batch, this is the one to skip: it costs almost the same as the ALFA AWUS036ACM ($40 vs $46.97), comes with no verifiable brand accountability, and inherits the same unresolved mt7921u injection bugs as the uConsole card - without that card's one real advantage of fitting a specific niche host. If you specifically want a WiFi 6/6E-class adapter for its own sake, this at least gives you a standalone dongle (unlike device 2), but for reliable monitor-mode/injection work, the MT7612U-based ALFA adapter remains the better buy at a similar price.",
