  "summary": "A $10-15 generic ESP32 development board with a 2.8-inch resistive touchscreen, microSD slot, RGB LED and speaker output, nicknamed the Cheap Yellow Display after its yellow PCB. It has become the default cheap platform for ESP32 security firmware: Bruce, GhostESP and HaleHound all ship builds for it, which makes it the honest price benchmark that every boutique handheld in this category should be compared against.",
  "honest_take": "This is the value benchmark: a $10-15 board with a colour touchscreen running exactly the same firmware as $150-280 boutique devices. The catches are real, though. Only three GPIOs are genuinely free - 22, 27 and 35, and 35 is input-only - so every 'full build' bolting on CC1101, nRF24, PN532 and GPS is sharing the display's SPI bus and juggling pin conflicts. There is no battery and no charging circuit, so it is a desk toy until you add both. The touch is resistive, not capacitive. And the ESP32-WROOM-32 has no USB peripheral at all - the USB port goes through a CH340 UART bridge - so BadUSB is permanently impossible on this board, no matter what firmware you flash.",
