  "stealth": "discreet",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "ESP32 Wardriver Pro is a solo-maintained, deliberately minimal wardriving logger: an ESP32 DevKit board, a GPS module, a small OLED and a microSD card, wired together and flashed with an Arduino sketch that scans Wi-Fi, waits for a GPS fix, and writes properly-formatted WigleWifi-1.4 CSV rows to the SD card. The README states outright that it does not deauthenticate, join networks, inject packets, or attack anything - it only watches and logs.",
  "honest_take": "The 'passive' framing is honest about intent but worth reading precisely: the underlying WiFi.scanNetworks() call is Arduino's standard active station-mode scan, which transmits 802.11 probe requests on every channel to elicit responses - the same thing your phone does constantly, not a promiscuous-mode radio that only listens. What 'passive' actually means here is narrower and true: no deauth, no packet injection, no joining networks, confirmed directly in the source (WifiScanner.cpp calls nothing but WiFi.scanNetworks). The upside of that narrow scope is real: this is a solo hobby project (40 stars) that still shipped exactly what it says it does - a genuine WigleWifi-1.4 CSV with a correct header - unlike wardriver3000 (also in this database), whose custom PCB and README both promise Wigle compatibility that its own firmware doesn't deliver. Against JCMK's actively-maintained 'C5 Wardriver' (dual-band 2.4+5GHz, BLE, also correctly WiGLE-formatted, from the same team behind ESP32 Marauder), this is the honest budget pick if you specifically want 2.4GHz-only passive logging and don't mind wiring your own board from loose modules - the C5 Wardriver adds 5GHz and BLE for not much more cost and effort, and has a larger, more active community behind it.",
