  "build_note": "No soldering to assemble, but the module is functionally inert until you build and flash firmware onto its ESP32-S3 (ESP-IDF 5.3+ per the mayhem-mdk repo) and pair it with a compatible PortaPack Mayhem build.",
  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "hat-shield",
  "summary": "A small ESP32-S3-based expansion board that attaches to a HackRF + PortaPack H4M rig over UART/I2C/USB, adding a second, independent Wi-Fi/BLE radio (the HackRF/PortaPack itself has no Wi-Fi) to an SDR platform that otherwise only receives/transmits via HackRF's wideband RF front end. It is an accessory for the existing 'HackRF + PortaPack H4M' device already tracked in this database, not a standalone gadget - it does nothing without that host.",
  "honest_take": "This is fundamentally 'an ESP32 dev board in a matching case' - the interesting part is entirely in whichever firmware you flash onto it, and the two options found are not equivalent: the official 'mayhem-mdk' reference firmware from the PortaPack Mayhem project only documents a single UART-passthrough demo app, while the real Wi-Fi functionality (an SSID-spam/spoofer app, plus IR-receive and a Meshtastic-related bridge) comes from a separate, single-maintainer community project (htotoo/ESP32-Portapack) that is explicitly described by its own author as early-stage. Marketing this as 'enable[s] WiFi applications' is accurate but easy to over-read: it does not turn the HackRF into a Wi-Fi attack tool in the Marauder/Flipper sense - deauth was not found documented anywhere in either firmware's app list, only beacon/SSID spam. You also need the PortaPack side running a Mayhem build recent enough to include the external-module API (PR #2290) or the app simply won't appear in the menu, which is a real trap for anyone still on an older Mayhem build or the original havoc fork. On price: the SKU 'OSL-ESP32' and matching product description point to Hacker Warehouse reselling OpenSourceSDRLab's own board, which OpenSourceSDRLab sells directly for as little as $35 on sale - so part of the $45 here is Hacker Warehouse's convenience/bundling markup, not unique hardware.",
