  "released": "2024",
  "price_usd": 8.0,
  "price_note": "No vendor product. A generic ESP32 dev board (plain ESP32, ESP32-C3 or ESP32-C6) costs roughly $5-10 on AliExpress/Amazon; there is nothing else to buy.",
  "price_is_estimate": true,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-10",
  "skill_level": "beginner",
  "skill_note": "PlatformIO or Arduino IDE flash onto a generic board; no soldering, no case.",
  "build_effort": "flash-only",
  "build_note": "Flash the firmware onto any generic ESP32/C3/C6 dev board over USB. No hardware assembly.",
  "stealth": "covert",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "Open-source firmware that spams spoofed BLE advertisements mimicking Apple's Continuity proximity-pairing protocol, triggering repeated fake AirPods/device-setup notification popups on nearby iPhones and iPads. It broadcasts 29 device-type adverts across 3 formats with randomized MAC addresses; with randomization tuned up it can produce a new notification on a target phone as fast as the previous one is dismissed.",
  "honest_take": "This is advertising-only - it broadcasts BLE packets, it does not scan or listen to anything, so calling it a 'sniffer' or a general BLE attack tool would be wrong. It's also iOS-specific and purely an annoyance/social-engineering vector: it cannot access data, install anything or bypass a lock screen, it just floods the Continuity protocol's UI with fake pairing prompts (the author notes opening the keyboard or camera app suppresses the popups). It runs on any $5-10 generic ESP32 - there's no proprietary hardware here, the $0 firmware is genuinely the whole product, and a Flipper Zero's WiFi Dev Board or any ESP32 add-on already in this database can run it just as well.",
  "best_for": [
   "Demonstrating BLE Continuity protocol abuse and iOS notification-spam social engineering in an authorized awareness/training context",
   "Learning BLE advertising packet crafting on cheap hardware"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Any form of BLE sniffing, scanning or data interception - it only transmits",
   "Attacking Android devices - targets Apple's Continuity protocol specifically",
   "Bypassing any security control - it is purely a UI/notification nuisance"
  ],
