  "price_note": "133 USD / 149.99 CAD. Product page read 'Sold out' on 2026-09-06 while the store's collection page showed it in stock - Shopify collection badges cache, so treat the product page as authoritative and expect intermittent restocks. US orders are quoted duties-included.",
  "price_is_estimate": false,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "advanced",
  "skill_note": "This is a Linux box you drive from a command line over a serial bridge. If you are not comfortable with Armbian, wireless drivers and monitor mode, most of it will be out of reach.",
  "build_effort": "flash-only",
  "build_note": "Accessory: needs a Flipper Zero as its screen, keypad and power source, plus a fast microSD card you supply (the docs are blunt that slow cards are the cause of nearly all boot problems - SanDisk Extreme / UHS-3 class or better). No Flipper and no SD card are included.",
  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "A quad-core Allwinner A33 Linux single-board computer in a Flipper Zero GPIO board shape, which borrows the Flipper's screen, buttons and battery and talks to it over the USB-UART bridge at 115200 baud. It runs an Armbian image with real Linux wireless tooling, and ships with a 5 GHz USB Wi-Fi dongle alongside its onboard 2.4 GHz radio.",
  "honest_take": "It is not an ESP32 board and comparing it to one misses the point - it is a 2014-era Android tablet SoC running Debian, which is both its strength (real aircrack-ng, real drivers, real scripting) and its weakness (512 MB RAM, a slow A7 cluster, and Wi-Fi that depends on Realtek out-of-tree drivers being happy). The 5 GHz capability everyone quotes comes from the bundled RTL8821CU USB dongle, not from the board's own RTL8723DS radio. Supply is the real problem: it sells out constantly, and the creator's hardware licence (CC BY-NC-SA) explicitly asks people not to resell boards, so there is no second source.",
  "best_for": [
   "Running genuine Linux wireless tooling with a Flipper as the interface",
   "People who want a scriptable Linux target rather than a menu-driven firmware",
   "Evil Twin and captive portal work with real hostapd/dnsmasq"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Anyone expecting ESP32-class instant-on simplicity",
   "Sub-GHz, NFC or 2.4 GHz non-Wi-Fi work - there is no CC1101, no nRF24, no GPS on it",
   "Buyers who need it in stock today"
  ],
