  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "The official Flipper Zero Wi-Fi Devboard (Flipper Devices' own $35 ESP32 add-on) modified by Texas Flipper Boards with a bolted-on GPS module and an external Wi-Fi antenna, reshipped in a custom 3D-printed enclosure and preloaded with the official ESP32 Marauder firmware. It has no storage or Bluetooth radio of its own - the upstream firmware's own board-feature table explicitly disables Bluetooth for this target - and wardriving logs (Wi-Fi scans tagged with GPS coordinates) are relayed over UART to the Flipper Zero's own microSD card.",
  "honest_take": "This is the official $35 Flipper Zero Wi-Fi Devboard with a GPS module and antenna added, resold for $120 - the markup buys someone else's soldering and a 3D-printed shell, not new silicon. It's also a direct head-to-head with BPM Circuits' NetNinja board, which costs $20 less ($100): the underlying Marauder firmware's own board-configuration file shows this board's stock 'MARAUDER_FLIPPER' target ships with Bluetooth explicitly disabled (`HAS_BT` is commented out), while the NetNinja's board target ships with it enabled. So the cheaper NetNinja is also, on paper, the more capable one - it can do BLE scanning and spam on top of the wardriving that both boards do, while this board is Wi-Fi- and GPS-only. What this board has going for it instead is that it tracks the current, actively-maintained upstream Marauder release (v1.16.0 at last check) rather than a vendor fork frozen years behind, so it should keep gaining features the NetNinja's stale fork won't. Neither board carries its own storage - both write to the Flipper's SD card - and as of September 2026 the seller has stopped taking orders, so it isn't purchasable right now regardless.",
