  "honest_take": "NetHunter itself is free and open source, and rooting a Nexus 5X and flashing it is a well-documented weekend project - what you are actually paying $120 for is someone else doing that setup, on hardware Google stopped patching in December 2018. That matters here specifically: this is a phone whose Android base has been unpatched for roughly eight years, running as your security-testing device, with all the exposure that implies for the device itself. On top of that, the Nexus 5X's internal Broadcom Wi-Fi chip is not reliably supported for monitor mode and packet injection in the wider NetHunter community's experience with this device class - most guides for phones like this recommend an external USB Wi-Fi adapter (an Alfa or similar RTL8812AU/RT3070-based unit) for real Wi-Fi cracking and deauth work, so 'WiFi cracking and deauthentication' as a bullet point is optimistic marketing rather than a guaranteed internal capability. If you can find any old, cheap, NetHunter-supported phone and 30 minutes of tutorial-following, you get the identical software for the cost of the used hardware alone.",
