  "build_note": "Accessory: requires a Flipper Zero on the GPIO header. Ships in a pre-installed two-piece 3D-printed enclosure with five antennas. The ESP32-C5 can also be flashed and run standalone over USB-C.",
  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "The most capable of the commodity Flipper multiboards: an ESP32-C5 with 5 GHz Wi-Fi running Marauder, two separate CC1101s dedicated to 433 MHz and 868 MHz with a hardware switch and indicator LEDs, an nRF24 for 2.4 GHz raw packet work, a GPS, and an SDXC slot, on five antennas. Documentation is unusually good for this market - a full manual with GPIO diagrams is published on GitHub.",
  "honest_take": "This is what the generic multiboard category looks like when someone actually engineers it, and the vendor's own documentation is refreshingly honest - it states plainly that Batch 1 could not do Marauder wardriving because the ESP32 UART and the GPS UART collided on the Flipper's pins, and that Batch 2 exists to fix exactly that. That is the whole story of this product class in one sentence: the chips are cheap and interchangeable, the pin routing is the hard part. Two caveats before you buy: it is chronically sold out, and dedicating one CC1101 to 868 MHz is a European-centric choice - if you are in North America, 915 MHz devices sit outside where that radio is matched.",
