  "honest_take": "It does one job and it is expensive for one job - $249 to find pinouts you could, with patience, find with a multimeter, an oscilloscope and a lot of time. Whether that is worth it depends entirely on how often you face an unlabelled board. For a professional doing regular teardowns it pays for itself the first week; for a hobbyist with one project it does not. Two practical limits: the target I/O voltage range is 1.4 V to 3.3 V, so 5 V targets need level shifting and very low-voltage cores may be out of range; and once it has found the interface you still need a Tigard, Glasgow or dedicated debugger to actually use it. It is a discovery tool, not a debugger.",
