  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "Basic keylogging is plug-and-forget; the match-triggered payloads that justify the price need real scripting and an understanding of USB attack modes.",
  "build_effort": "assembled",
  "build_note": "Plug the keyboard into the Croc, the Croc into the computer. Press the hidden button with a paperclip to enter arming mode and edit config.txt.",
  "stealth": "covert",
  "form_factor": "usb-stick",
  "summary": "A hardware keylogger that is also a small Linux computer. It sits between a USB keyboard and the host, logs every keystroke, and - crucially - can watch that stream for keywords or regular expressions and fire a payload when one appears. It can then switch USB personalities to a keyboard, flash drive, serial device or Ethernet adapter to act on what it saw.",
  "honest_take": "The keyword-matching trigger is the genuinely novel bit and nothing cheap does it: a $10 hardware keylogger records to a chip you have to come back and retrieve, whereas this can notice someone typing a password and immediately react over Wi-Fi. Two things to be clear about. First, despite the price and the marketing, this is DuckyScript 2.0, not 3 - commands are QUACK-prefixed and you do not get the compiled language's variables, conditionals, loops or Keystroke Reflection exfiltration. Second, Hak5's own specification block lists no CPU, no RAM and no SoC; the 'quad-core 1.2 GHz ARM, 512 MB RAM' figures you will see quoted come from marketing copy and third-party wikis, not a published spec or a teardown. There is also a well-reported operational footgun: logs are flushed to storage when you enter arming mode, so pulling it out of the port without arming it first can cost you the capture.",
  "best_for": [
   "Physical-access engagements where you can reach a keyboard cable",
   "Triggering an attack the moment a specific credential or keyword is typed",
   "Live remote keystroke monitoring via Cloud C2",
   "Demonstrating why USB port access is a security boundary"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Bluetooth or laptop built-in keyboards - it needs a wired USB keyboard in the path",
   "DuckyScript 3 payloads",
   "Anyone who cannot physically reach and later retrieve the device",
   "Situations where an unfamiliar dongle on the keyboard cable would be noticed"
  ],
