  "summary": "An RFM95 LoRa radio backpack (selectable 868 or 915 MHz, up to +20 dBm/100 mW) that plugs onto Retia's USB Nugget or Bluetooth Nugget BadUSB platforms, letting a Nugget receive and execute DuckyScript-style keystroke-injection payloads over a long-range LoRa link instead of only Wi-Fi/Bluetooth.",
  "honest_take": "This is a range extender for an existing attack platform, not an attack tool by itself - the actual BadUSB/keystroke-injection capability lives entirely in the Nugget it plugs into; this board just adds a LoRa command-and-control channel. Retia's own marketing line, 'send BadUSB commands from miles away,' is technically true of LoRa's point-to-point range in ideal open-field, line-of-sight conditions (the RFM95's own datasheet-class figures are commonly cited around 2km with simple antennas, up to double digits of km with directional antennas), but real-world range through walls and buildings during a physical-access engagement will be a small fraction of that - treat 'miles' as a lab number, not an operational one. The RFM95 is a well-understood, off-the-shelf LoRa module also used in Retia's own Nibble boards; nothing about the chip itself is novel.",
