  "firmware_repo": "https://github.com/7h30th3r0n3/Evil-M5Project",
  "firmware_note": "Same repo also ships Evil-Cardputer (the author's recommended primary target, with the deepest feature set: BadUSB, IMSI catcher, LLM chat, IR toolkit, NTLMv2 cracking, SIP/CCTV toolkits), Evil-AtomS3 (needs a separate GPS module, reduced UI on the tiny screen), Evil-M5Core3, and a beta CYD/M5Stick build. This record covers the Core2 build, which per the project's own feature matrix has roughly half of Cardputer's modules and lacks BadUSB, IMSI catcher, LLM chat, IR, and several LAN-attack tools Cardputer has.",
  "vendor": "M5Stack",
  "vendor_url": "https://m5stack.com",
  "product_url": "https://shop.m5stack.com/products/m5stack-core2-esp32-iot-development-kit-v1-3",
  "category": "wifi",
  "subcategory": "wifi-bluetooth-audit",
  "status": "available",
  "released": "2023",
  "price_usd": 42.9,
  "price_note": "The firmware itself is free. $42.90 is M5Stack's current price for the Core2 v1.3 hardware it runs on (the older v1.1 unit is now EOL and redirects buyers to v1.3); add about $12.50 for M5Stack's own GPS/BDS unit if you want the wardriving feature. No vendor sells this pre-flashed as an 'Evil-M5' product.",
  "price_is_estimate": true,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-10",
  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "M5Burner's web flasher covers basic installation in a couple of minutes, but the project's own instructions require pinning an old ESP32 Arduino core (2.1.4 or below), running a separate script to bypass Espressif's deauth guard, adding extra libraries for BadUSB, and placing SD content inside a specifically-named 'evil' folder - none of that is beginner-friendly.",
  "build_effort": "flash-only",
  "build_note": "No soldering for the Core2 build itself - M5Burner or the Arduino IDE flashes over USB-C. The optional GPS unit for wardriving is a Grove-port plug-in, not a solder job.",
  "stealth": "discreet",
  "form_factor": "handheld",
  "summary": "Evil-M5Project is a free Wi-Fi/BLE pentest firmware, MIT-licensed per its README (though GitHub finds no separate LICENSE file to auto-detect), that the author builds for several M5Stack boards - M5Cardputer, Core2, Core3, AtomS3 and M5Stack Fire - from one shared codebase. On Core2 it covers scanning, deauth, captive-portal credential capture, karma attacks, beacon spam, handshake capture, a basic LAN toolkit (rogue DHCP/DNS, port scanning), and Flipper/skimmer/deauth detection; the fuller module set (BadUSB, IMSI catcher, LLM chat, IR) is Cardputer-only.",
  "honest_take": "This is firmware for the plain ESP32 inside a Core2, not a purpose-built security device - the same Xtensa LX6 silicon is in $5 dev boards, and the $42.90 buys you the screen, battery, case and Grove ports, not any special radio capability. The project's own README recommends the Cardputer over the Core2 ('Better one: M5Cardputer'), and its own feature matrix confirms it: Core2 gets deauth, karma, captive portals and a basic LAN toolkit, but not BadUSB, IMSI catching, IR or the 'Wardriving Master' multi-node mode. The 'Evil-Twin' checkbox itself is unchecked for Core2 in that same matrix - despite having network cloning, karma and a captive portal, the developer doesn't consider the automated evil-twin chain complete on this target. Deauth and beacon spam only reach unprotected management frames - WPA3's mandatory PMF stops it like every other ESP32 tool. And the credential-harvesting captive portal plus rogue-DHCP/DNS features push this well past 'harmless Wi-Fi scanner' territory some buyers expect - using them against a network you don't own is a computer-misuse-shaped decision, not a grey area.",
  "best_for": [
   "Learning 802.11 attack mechanics on hardware you already own",
   "Wi-Fi recon, karma and captive-portal testing in a lab you control",
   "A cheaper, screen-equipped alternative to a bare ESP32 dev board for a Marauder-adjacent feature set"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Anything needing sub-GHz, NFC/RFID or a genuine IR blaster - Core2 has none of that in this firmware",
   "Deauthing modern PMF/WPA3 networks",
   "The project's fullest feature set - that's the Cardputer build, not Core2",
   "Anyone wanting a supported, warrantied product - this is a hobby repo with no tagged releases, just a rolling main branch"
  ],
