  "firmware_repo": "https://github.com/ElectronicCats/Minino",
  "firmware_note": "GPL-3.0 firmware with a menu of Wi-Fi, BLE, Zigbee, GPS and surveillance-detection apps; OTA updates supported.",
  "vendor": "Electronic Cats",
  "vendor_url": "https://electroniccats.com",
  "product_url": "https://electroniccats.com/store/minino/",
  "category": "multitool",
  "subcategory": "iot-multitool",
  "status": "available",
  "released": "2024",
  "price_usd": 65.0,
  "price_note": "USD 65 direct; DEF CON edition variants USD 70-75; Lab401 EUR 65. Needs a microSD (not included) and 3x AAA.",
  "price_is_estimate": false,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "beginner",
  "skill_note": "Menu-driven on a small OLED; no host computer needed for most functions.",
  "build_effort": "assembled",
  "build_note": "Comes assembled with antennas and battery holder.",
  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "pocket",
  "summary": "An ESP32-C6 pocket tool with GPS, OLED and microSD that covers 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi, BLE and 802.15.4 (Zigbee/Thread/Matter) from one menu: wardriving, deauth, beacon spam, BLE tracker detection, Zigbee sniffing to Wireshark, Remote ID drone decoding and a Flock Safety ALPR/body-cam detector. It is the closest thing to a Flipper for the IoT/2.4 GHz side.",
  "honest_take": "It is an ESP32-C6 in a nice case with a GPS bolted on - there is no sub-GHz radio, no NFC/RFID and no infrared, so it is not a Flipper replacement. Everything it does is 2.4 GHz plus GNSS. The 'surveillance detection' features are pattern matching on Wi-Fi/BLE identifiers of known camera and ALPR gear, so they find kit that advertises itself and miss anything that does not.",
