  "firmware_kind": "diy",
  "firmware_repo": "https://github.com/cifertech/nRFBox",
  "firmware_note": "Current firmware line (version numbering runs ahead of the hardware revision): scanner, analyser, channel jammer, BLE jammer, BLE spoofer, Sour Apple, Proto Kill, plus Wi-Fi and BLE scanners and a Wi-Fi deauther added in 2.7.2.",
  "vendor": "CiferTech (open-source project, no official vendor)",
  "vendor_url": "https://cifertech.net/",
  "product_url": "https://github.com/cifertech/nRFBox",
  "category": "multitool",
  "subcategory": "24ghz-multitool",
  "status": "diy-only",
  "released": "2025",
  "price_usd": 50.0,
  "price_note": "ESTIMATE, parts only. ESP32-WROOM-32U ~$6, 3x nRF24L01+ (PA/LNA variants ~$4 each) ~$8-12, SH1106/SSD1306 OLED ~$4, NeoPixel, buttons and passives ~$4, charger and regulator ~$2, LiPo cell ~$6, antennas and pigtails ~$8. Custom PCB from the repo gerbers at JLCPCB minimum quantity 5, ~$12-20 shipped. One finished unit realistically $55-70. Note the power caveat the project itself documents: running three nRF24 modules at once caused startup crashes until it was fixed in firmware 2.1.1, and cheap modules with poor decoupling still cause it.",
  "price_is_estimate": true,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "Custom PCB, three radio modules and an OLED; you must be comfortable soldering, flashing an ESP32, and troubleshooting brownouts when all three radios transmit together.",
  "build_effort": "soldering",
  "build_note": "Gerbers, schematics and pre-compiled binaries are all in the repo, and 2.7.2 added firmware updating from an SD card. Assemble, flash over USB, done. Nothing is sold assembled by the author.",
  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "handheld",
  "summary": "The current nRFBox: an ESP32-WROOM-32U with three nRF24L01+ modules and an OLED, combining passive 2.4GHz spectrum scanning and analysis with active BLE and multi-protocol disruption, plus Wi-Fi and BLE scanning and a deauther that use the ESP32's own radio. It is the more useful sibling of RF-Clown - same jamming ability, but with the eyes to see what you are doing.",
  "honest_take": "The honest split here is that half this device is a legitimate, genuinely useful 2.4GHz spectrum tool and the other half is a jammer that is illegal to switch on almost everywhere. Understand which mode you are in. The 'Proto Kill' name oversells it: eight protocols in a menu is still three nRF24s putting narrowband energy on a handful of 1MHz channels, and it does not speak Zigbee or Wi-Fi any more than a bullhorn speaks French. 'Sour Apple' is BLE advertisement spam that makes iPhone popups appear - an annoyance, not a compromise. And the deauther inherits the usual limit: 2.4GHz only, and useless against any network with 802.11w/PMF enabled, which WPA3 mandates.",
  "best_for": [
   "Seeing 2.4GHz channel occupancy on a screen for about $50",
   "Learning the difference between energy detection and protocol decoding",
   "A well-supported intermediate PCB build with active maintenance"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "5GHz anything",
   "Decoding Zigbee, Wi-Fi or Bluetooth traffic",
   "Legal operation of its jamming modes",
   "Cracking or capturing Wi-Fi credentials"
  ],
