  "released": "2026",
  "price_usd": 140.0,
  "price_note": "USD 140 is the implied retail price - both single-unit Kickstarter tiers advertise their discount against it (79 USD super early bird, 99 USD early bird). Careful: the 140 USD figure on the campaign is also the price of a two-unit bundle, which is probably where the sheet's number came from. Kickstarter ran 14 January to 13 February 2026, raising 62,537 USD from 591 backers against a 10,000 USD goal. As of late August 2026 the campaign status is BETA with the first batch shipping and the second batch pending. You cannot buy one: the vendor site has no cart, only an availability waitlist.",
  "price_is_estimate": false,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "Four very different modes on one small mono OLED, plus a Lua scripting layer and a CLI. Approachable to poke at, deep to actually use.",
  "build_effort": "assembled",
  "build_note": "Ships assembled; there is a web flasher and the firmware builds from source with ESP-IDF v6.1.",
  "stealth": "discreet",
  "form_factor": "pocket",
  "summary": "A small ESP32-C5 handheld with a 1.3in monochrome OLED, a D-pad and A/B buttons, a Qwiic connector, microSD, an IMU, an IR transmitter and an NFC front end. It splits into four personalities: a maker mode with an I2C scanner and sensor streaming into Home Assistant or Edge Impulse, a motion-to-MIDI performance mode, a genuine wireless security toolkit covering Wi-Fi, BLE, Zigbee and Thread with PCAP export, and an Arduboy-compatible games machine that runs the whole Arduboy library.",
  "honest_take": "The security half is more real than the price suggests - the firmware carries a working PicoPass/iCLASS reader with loclass key recovery and Wiegand decoding, MIFARE Classic key discovery, an 802.15.4 sniffer that writes PCAP, Open Drone ID capture and spoofing, and Lua scripting. That is a serious amount of engineering for a 140-dollar gadget. But three things do not line up. The site says MIT licence while the actual repository has no licence file at all, which legally means all rights reserved. The Kickstarter says the default NFC chip is a PN532 with the far more capable ST25R3916 only as a stretch goal, while the firmware's entire NFC stack is written against the ST25R3916 - so which chip is in a shipped unit is genuinely unclear. And the repo's own default build target is the ESP32-C6, which is 2.4 GHz only, even though the whole product is marketed on the C5's 5 GHz. There is also no sub-GHz radio at all, no published battery capacity, and no way to actually order one.",
  "best_for": [
   "Zigbee, Thread and 802.15.4 reconnaissance with PCAP export, which almost nothing else at this price does",
   "PicoPass/iCLASS and MIFARE Classic work on a budget",
   "Makers who want an I2C/Qwiic sensor handheld that also plays the Arduboy library",
   "Motion-to-MIDI performance, which is a genuinely unusual feature"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Sub-GHz - there is no CC1101, Si446x or any other sub-GHz radio",
   "Anyone who needs to buy one today; it is waitlist-only",
   "Buyers who need a licence they can rely on - the firmware has none",
   "Anyone assuming 5 GHz just works, given the repo builds for the 2.4GHz-only C6 by default"
  ],
