  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "Flashing an image and editing a YAML config is the easy part; the hard part is that the Wi-Fi driver is a patched firmware blob (nexmon) tied to a specific kernel, so when something breaks you are debugging kernel modules on a headless Pi over USB gadget mode.",
  "build_effort": "assembly",
  "build_note": "No soldering if you buy a Pi Zero 2 WH with the header pre-fitted and a HAT-style e-ink display - it is a stack-and-flash build. Soldering is only needed if you buy the headerless Pi. Flash the fork's image to the card, boot, connect over USB gadget mode.",
  "stealth": "discreet",
  "form_factor": "pocket",
  "summary": "A Raspberry Pi in a small case with an e-ink screen that walks around collecting WPA handshake material. It drives bettercap to capture PMKIDs and full or half WPA handshakes into PCAPNG files for later cracking with hashcat, and shows an animated face on the display reflecting what it is doing. Multiple units in range advertise their presence to each other using custom information elements in 802.11 beacons.",
  "honest_take": "Two things people get wrong. First, the AI: the maintained fork removed it entirely - the maintainer's README says AI destabilised the Wi-Fi firmware, so it was cut for uptime and battery life. Even when present, it was reinforcement learning tuning channel-hop and dwell parameters, not anything that understood networks. Second, it does not crack anything. It collects handshake material; the actual cracking happens later on a real GPU, and if the target's password is not in your wordlist you get nothing at all. Add that most of what it captures is from networks you have no permission to touch, and the honest description is 'a Tamagotchi that generates hashcat homework'.",
