  "summary": "RFIDler is Aperture Labs' (Adam Laurie) open-hardware, software-defined LF (125/134kHz) reader/writer/emulator built around a Microchip PIC32MX795F512L, controlled over a USB-serial CLI. Firmware source confirms real read, write and one-shot emulate (EMU command) support for a broad sweep of LF formats including EM4X02/4X05, HID Prox (HID26), Indala (64/224-bit), UNIQUE, HITAG1/2, Q5, T55x7, AWID26 and FDXB, with write support specifically for T55x7, Q5 and HITAG2. It has no HF hardware at all.",
  "honest_take": "This is a decade-plus-old project: Aperture Labs' own store lists it at £80+VAT 'Currently OUT OF STOCK' with no restock date, and the last real firmware commit was September 2024 - treat it as effectively DIY-only now, buildable from the Eagle files in the repo rather than something you can simply order. It's also not standalone in the field: it needs a host PC/terminal over USB the entire time you're using it, unlike a Proxmark3 or Flipper Zero. If you can get or build one, the format coverage is genuinely broad for LF and the emulate command is real (confirmed directly in firmware source, not just marketing copy) - but a Proxmark3 or Flipper Zero will get a newcomer working today with far more active support.",
