  "price_usd": 199.0,
  "price_note": "Approximately $199 at last listing, but this figure is from secondary sources only - the product has been fully delisted, its shop page returns 404, and it appears on Hak5's official discontinued list. Only available secondhand.",
  "price_is_estimate": true,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "advanced",
  "skill_note": "There is no UI at all. Everything is a bash payload written blind and dropped on a USB stick, with a single LED for feedback.",
  "build_effort": "assembled",
  "build_note": "Flash the current firmware from a USB stick on first boot, then drop payloads on the stick.",
  "stealth": "covert",
  "form_factor": "usb-stick",
  "summary": "A headless Wi-Fi attack platform on a USB-A pigtail with a pass-through USB port, designed to be plugged in behind a monitor or into a USB charger and left running. It has no screen and no web interface: you write a bash payload, put it on a flash drive, and read a single LED. Discontinued.",
  "honest_take": "Two hard facts settle this one. It was discontinued and delisted by Hak5, and its payload repository has not been touched since September 2020 - the openwrt-owl repo is archived. The hardware is a 2019-era Atheros AR9331 at 400 MHz with 64 MB of RAM and a single 2.4 GHz radio at 0.1 W, which we confirmed by reading the die markings in the FCC internal photos: ATHEROS AR9331-AL1A alongside an ESMT M13S512168A. So it is 2.4 GHz only, single-core MIPS, dead software, no vendor support, and secondhand only. Everything it did, a $15 Raspberry Pi Zero W or an ESP32 board with a USB power passthrough will do better today with maintained software. Its one genuinely clever design touch was the pass-through USB port: with a keyboard or mouse plugged through it, the Owl itself never enumerates on the target OS.",
  "best_for": [
   "Historical interest and secondhand lab use",
   "Long unattended 2.4 GHz collection from a USB power bank (roughly four days on a 20,000 mAh bank)",
   "Understanding the design lineage of the Pineapple Pager"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Any new purchase - it is discontinued and delisted",
   "5 GHz or 6 GHz work",
   "Anyone who wants maintained firmware",
   "Anyone who wants a UI of any kind"
  ],
