  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "advanced",
  "skill_note": "Cheap and capable, but you must flash it yourself over its serial bootloader and then interpret BLE link-layer traffic in Wireshark.",
  "build_effort": "flash-only",
  "build_note": "Open the aluminium case, hold the BOOT button while plugging in, flash the Sniffle image with cc2538-bsl. Reversible - you can flash it back to Zigbee later.",
  "stealth": "covert",
  "form_factor": "usb-stick",
  "summary": "A $20 Zigbee dongle whose TI CC2652P is exactly the silicon NCC Group's Sniffle targets. Flashed with Sniffle it captures BLE 5 traffic across all three primary advertising channels simultaneously, supports every BLE 5 PHY including coded, and follows connections far more reliably than single-channel sniffers.",
  "honest_take": "This is the answer to 'what should I actually buy to sniff BLE', and almost nobody knows it. Watching all three advertising channels at once is not a nice-to-have - it is the difference between catching the connection request and missing it, and Sniffle's own documentation puts connection detection at roughly three times more reliable than single-channel sniffers. It beats a retired $125 Ubertooth and a $11 Nordic dongle on capability while sitting between them on price. The catches are real but small: you must flash it yourself, doing so removes the Zigbee function, and Sniffle is a link-layer capture tool - it does not break encryption, and a connection using LE Secure Connections pairing will be captured but not readable.",
  "best_for": [
   "The best value BLE sniffing setup available",
   "Capturing BLE 5 extended advertising and coded PHY traffic",
   "Reliably following BLE connections",
   "Wireshark-based BLE analysis"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Bluetooth Classic - the CC2652P does not do BR/EDR",
   "Decrypting BLE - Sniffle captures, it does not break crypto",
   "Anyone unwilling to flash firmware over a serial bootloader",
   "Keeping your Zigbee network running on the same dongle"
  ],
