  "status": "diy-only",
  "released": "2024",
  "price_usd": 25.0,
  "price_note": "No commercial seller. Estimate covers PCB fabrication plus an ESP32-C3, RS485 transceiver and connectors; the maintainer offers free boards on request and shares the design through PCBWay.",
  "price_is_estimate": true,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "expert",
  "skill_note": "You fabricate and hand-solder the board, then install it inside live access-control wiring.",
  "build_effort": "soldering",
  "build_note": "Revision 0.2 was deliberately redesigned with a SOIC-8 RS485 part so it can be hand-soldered without stencils or hot air.",
  "stealth": "implant",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "The most capable open-source access-control implant currently maintained: an ESP32-C3 board that sits behind a card reader and handles Wiegand, magnetic-stripe clock-and-data and some OSDP, with both BLE and Wi-Fi interfaces, configurable data lines, and tolerance for up to 25 V supply and 16 V data lines. Its own README contains an honest comparison table against BLEKey, ESP-RFID-Tool and ESPKey.",
  "honest_take": "The maintainer describes his own firmware as 'slightly-organised code soup' - this is a research project, not a product, and there is nobody to call when it misbehaves inside a client's wall. It is also the only one of the four Wiegand implants that is still under active development, which matters more than polish. The OSDP support is partial: do not assume it defeats a properly configured OSDP Secure Channel installation.",
  "best_for": [
   "Red teams that can build their own hardware",
   "Sites using magstripe clock-and-data as well as Wiegand",
   "Anyone who wants a maintained alternative to the abandoned BLEKey"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Anyone who cannot solder or fabricate PCBs",
   "Properly encrypted OSDP deployments",
   "Deployment without written authorisation"
  ],
