  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "The hardware is genuinely simple - switches for voltage and mode - but you need to know which host tool to point at it and what a JTAG scan chain is.",
  "build_effort": "assembled",
  "build_note": "Assembled board with labelled headers. Case optional. USB-C.",
  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "An FT2232HQ-based multi-protocol, multi-voltage adapter with proper level shifters and dedicated labelled headers for UART, JTAG, SWD, SPI and I2C. Its whole reason to exist is that it handles 1.8 V to 5.5 V correctly and does not require you to guess which pin is which.",
  "honest_take": "This is the boring, correct answer for most hardware hacking, and the honest pitch is that it saves you from a drawer full of cheap FTDI breakouts that all run at 3.3 V and quietly damage 1.8 V targets. The 1.8 V support is the point - a lot of modern flash and SoCs are 1.8 V and the generic adapters simply do not handle them. Its limits are real though: FT2232H means two channels, so you get one dedicated UART plus one shared port that must be switched between SPI/JTAG and I2C/SWD, and its 'logic analyzer port' is a header for your own analyser, not a built-in one. It does not discover interfaces for you either - that is a JTAGulator.",
  "best_for": [
   "Any target running at 1.8 V",
   "UART, JTAG, SWD, SPI and I2C from one board with labelled headers",
   "Driving OpenOCD, flashrom, UrJTAG and pyftdi",
   "The single most sensible first purchase for hardware debug work"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Discovering unknown pinouts - use a JTAGulator",
   "High-speed capture or protocol analysis - use Glasgow",
   "Doing two shared-port protocols at the same time",
   "Glitching or side-channel work"
  ],
