  "honest_take": "This gets filed under 'SDR' for lack of a dedicated test-equipment category, but calling it an SDR is misleading - a tinySA sweeps frequency and shows you signal strength, it cannot demodulate, decode, or record raw I/Q like a HackRF or RTL-SDR can. Its real strength (finding an unknown transmitter's exact frequency before you point a proper SDR at it) is genuinely useful for RF security work, but the flashy 5.4GHz+ 'Ultra mode' numbers are uncalibrated and 10GHz harmonic mode is a mathematical trick using higher-order mixer harmonics with reduced accuracy - the honestly-usable calibrated range is 100kHz-900MHz. Multiple hardware revisions exist under similar names (ZS406 vs ZS407, this listing is the ZS406, capped at 5.4GHz Ultra mode; the ZS407 variant goes to 7.3GHz) - confirm which one you're buying, since sellers aren't always precise about it.",
