  "released": "2021",
  "price_usd": 99.0,
  "price_note": "Two tiers seen from the original Crowd Supply campaign: $89 for the board plus NFC antenna, $99 for the kit adding a 3D-printed enclosure; add $8 US / $18 international shipping. The campaign funded in 2021-2022 but backers reported shipping delays into 2023 over Texas Instruments component sourcing for the RF430CL330 NFC chip. Current lead time for a new order in 2026 was not confirmed.",
  "price_is_estimate": true,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-10",
  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "Setup involves loading an OSM-derived camera-location database onto the SD card and pairing with the companion Android app over NFC - not a flash-and-forget device.",
  "build_effort": "assembled",
  "build_note": "Ships assembled, 45mm x 32mm PCB. Fully open hardware if you would rather build your own from the published KiCad files.",
  "stealth": "discreet",
  "form_factor": "pocket",
  "summary": "A tiny open-source ESP32 board with a real GNSS receiver, an accelerometer and a passive NFC tag interface. It logs a GPS track, cross-references it offline against an OpenStreetMap-derived database of known camera locations stored on its SD card, and produces a daily proximity report read by tapping a phone's NFC reader against it - no data leaves the device to any third party.",
  "honest_take": "This is a location logger with an offline lookup table, not a camera detector - it does not sense cameras with any sensor of its own; it matches a GPS track against wherever OSM contributors have manually tagged a camera, so its accuracy is entirely a function of how complete and current that crowdsourced tagging is in a given area, which varies enormously by city and is frequently stale or missing entirely for private or newer installations. The privacy design is genuinely good and rare in this category - fully open source, a passive NFC tag rather than an active radio to avoid leaking the device's presence, and everything processed locally rather than phoning home - but don't confuse 'privacy-respecting camera awareness log' with 'camera detector.' It also shipped years late to its original 2021-2022 Crowd Supply backers over component sourcing problems, worth weighing against a small team with no dedicated production line.",
  "best_for": [
   "Privacy-conscious commute/route logging against an offline camera database",
   "Anyone who wants a fully open-source, auditable design in this category rather than a closed app"
  ],
  "not_for": [
   "Real-time camera detection - it does no RF or visual sensing, only offline database lookup against a logged GPS track",
   "Areas with sparse OSM camera tagging, where the report will simply be incomplete",
   "Buyers who need it fast - the project has a documented history of significant shipping delays"
  ],
