  "summary": "VICINO is a bare SPI breakout board built around ST's ST25R3916B multi-protocol 13.56MHz NFC/RFID reader IC, designed by UK studio MCQN Ltd as a mechanically and electrically compatible upgrade path for projects built around cheap MFRC522 (RC522) modules. It was originally developed for MCQN's own Museum in a Box v2.0 project, ships assembled and OSHWA-certified (UK000061), and pairs with MCQN's forked ST NFC-RFAL/ST25R3916 Arduino libraries running on an ESP32 host.",
  "honest_take": "This is not a pentest tool - it's a better-spec'd reader chip in an RC522-shaped footprint, built for a museum tag-reading kiosk, not for card attacks; don't expect Mifare Classic cracking or Flipper-style attack menus out of the box. You still bring your own host MCU, wire SPI yourself, and build firmware against a small (11-star), still-thin-on-docs library fork rather than a mature SDK. At $40 for a chip that street-prices around $8-12 bare from distributors, the premium buys the antenna-tuned PCB, RC522 pin/hole compatibility and OSHW documentation - reasonable if that compatibility saves you a redesign, questionable if you're just comparing raw part cost.",
