  "skill_note": "You fabricate the author's custom PCB yourself (Altium source files only - most hobbyists will need Altium's free viewer, not their usual KiCad workflow, just to inspect it) and hand-solder an ESP32, GPS module, OLED and SD breakout to it, then compile Arduino code with pins hardcoded in the .ino rather than a config file.",
  "build_effort": "soldering",
  "build_note": "Custom two-layer PCB (Gerbers not separately provided - only Altium source files plus top/bottom photos) and a 3D-printed case (STL files included). No kit or assembled option exists.",
  "stealth": "discreet",
  "form_factor": "pocket",
  "summary": "wardriver3000 is a solo hobbyist's open-source ESP32 + GPS + OLED wardriving rig on a custom PCB: it scans nearby Wi-Fi networks, tags them with a GPS fix, and appends the results to a CSV file on a microSD card. It's a from-scratch build - custom board, 3D-printed case, no assembled version - for people who want to build their own wardriving hardware rather than buy one.",
  "honest_take": "The GitHub repo has had zero commits since April 28, 2024 - over two years before this was written - and a basic 'can you give the gpio pins for this project' issue from mid-2024 sits open and unanswered, so treat this as a frozen snapshot, not a maintained project. More importantly, despite the README's framing around uploading to Wigle.net, the actual firmware writes a custom CSV (BSSID, SSID, capabilities, RSSI, channel, lat, lng, then three hardcoded zeros for altitude/accuracy/type) with no WigleWifi-1.4 header line at all - that's not the format WiGLE's own importer expects, so you'd need to reformat the file yourself before uploading, something the README never mentions. The scanning itself is also just the stock Arduino WiFi.scanNetworks() station-mode call - the same thing any $5 ESP32 dev board can do - not a promiscuous-mode capture; nothing here actually needed the custom PCB except tidiness. For similar money and real solder time, JCMK's actively-maintained 'C5 Wardriver' (dual-band 2.4+5GHz plus BLE, genuine WiGLE-formatted output, updated within the last month as of this check) is the better wardriving pick if you're choosing between DIY projects, and this database's ESP32 Wardriver Pro is a more honestly-documented single-band alternative at similar build effort.",
