  "form_factor": "box",
  "summary": "An ESP32-S3-based OBD-II scanner built around a dedicated (unnamed by the vendor) OBD interpreter chip that covers all legislated OBD-II protocols in one device: ISO 15765-4 CAN, SAE J1939 CAN, ISO 11898 raw CAN, Medium-Speed CAN (MS-CAN), GM High-Speed CAN, Single-Wire CAN (GMLAN), plus the legacy K-line protocols SAE J1850-PWM/VPW, ISO 9141-2, and ISO 14230-4. It emulates ELM327 v2.3, ELM329 v2.2, STN, and VT instruction sets (so it works with Car Scanner, Torque, OBD Auto Doctor, BimmerCode, OBD Fusion, etc.), integrates directly with Home Assistant with no companion app required, and adds a USB-C flashing port, microSD logging, a wake-on-motion accelerometer, and a USB host port for optional LTE/GPS modules on top of the base WiCAN feature set.",
  "honest_take": "The protocol coverage genuinely is broad - MeatPi's own comparison table shows it matching or beating a $150+ OBDLink MX+ on paper - but CAN-FD is never mentioned anywhere in the spec sheet despite being increasingly common on 2021+ vehicles, and there's no claim of galvanic isolation either, so this is a convenience/diagnostics tool for your own vehicle's OBD-II port, not a lab-grade isolated interface for bench work on a live harness. The 'cannot be powered via USB-C' caveat catches people off guard too: you can't bench-test it from a USB power brick, only from a vehicle or an OBD-II 12V breakout.",
